  "worked_solution": "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $5$ and subtracting $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4})\\le -5 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4})-3\\le -5-3 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $5$ and subtracting $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4})\\ge 5 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4})-3\\ge 5-3 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -8,\\,f(x)\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$",
